# West - Nutrition and Wellness for Life (Goodheart-Willcox).
# Chapter list: 6th edition (2023), from the publisher's table of contents (g-w.com).
# Summaries cover each chapter's standard topics; they are written for this site, not copied from the book.

def ch(n, title, text, keys):
    return (n, title, [text], keys)

C = [
    ch(1, "Making Wellness a Lifestyle", "Wellness is a state of being that includes physical, mental, social, emotional, and other dimensions. Lifestyle choices—diet, activity, sleep, avoiding tobacco—affect risk of chronic diseases such as heart disease, type 2 diabetes, and cancer. Making changes involves setting SMART goals.", ["Dimensions of wellness", "Lifestyle affects chronic disease", "SMART goals", "Prevention"]),
    ch(2, "Factors Affecting Food Habits", "Food choices are shaped by culture, family, religion, emotions, social influences, media and advertising, cost, and availability. Hunger is the physical need for food; appetite is the psychological desire to eat.", ["Hunger vs. appetite", "Culture and family", "Media influence", "Cost and availability"]),
    ch(3, "Practicing Safe Food Habits", "Foodborne illness is caused by pathogens such as Salmonella, E. coli, and Listeria. Prevent it with the four steps: clean, separate, cook (to safe internal temperatures, e.g., poultry 165°F), and chill (refrigerate within 2 hours). The temperature danger zone is 40–140°F.", ["Clean, separate, cook, chill", "Danger zone 40–140°F", "Poultry 165°F", "Refrigerate within 2 hours"]),
    ch(4, "Nutrients and You", "The six nutrient classes are carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, vitamins, minerals, and water. Energy-yielding nutrients provide calories: carbohydrates and protein (4 cal/g), fat (9 cal/g); alcohol gives 7 cal/g. Digestion breaks food down; absorption mostly occurs in the small intestine.", ["Six nutrient classes", "Carbs/protein 4 cal/g", "Fat 9 cal/g", "Small intestine absorbs"]),
    ch(5, "Nutrition Guidelines", "Tools include the Dietary Guidelines for Americans, MyPlate (fruits, vegetables, grains, protein, dairy), Dietary Reference Intakes (RDA, AI, UL), and the Nutrition Facts label (serving size and % Daily Value; 5% DV is low, 20% is high).", ["MyPlate", "Dietary Guidelines", "DRIs: RDA, AI, UL", "%DV: 5% low, 20% high"]),
    ch(6, "Carbohydrates", "Carbohydrates are the body's main energy source. Simple carbs are sugars; complex carbs include starch and fiber. Fiber (soluble and insoluble) aids digestion and lowers cholesterol. Limit added sugars. Glucose is stored as glycogen.", ["Main energy source", "Simple vs. complex", "Fiber benefits", "Limit added sugars"]),
    ch(7, "Lipids: Fats and Oils", "Lipids include triglycerides, phospholipids, and sterols (cholesterol). Saturated fats (solid at room temperature, mostly animal) and trans fats raise LDL ('bad') cholesterol; unsaturated fats (mono and poly; oils) are healthier. Omega-3 fatty acids support heart health. HDL is 'good' cholesterol.", ["Saturated and trans fats raise LDL", "Unsaturated fats healthier", "Omega-3s", "HDL is good"]),
    ch(8, "Proteins", "Proteins are made of amino acids; nine are essential and must come from food. Complete proteins (animal foods, soy) contain all essential amino acids; incomplete proteins can be combined (complementary proteins). Protein builds and repairs tissue. Deficiency causes kwashiorkor or marasmus.", ["20 amino acids, 9 essential", "Complete vs. incomplete", "Complementary proteins", "Build and repair"]),
    ch(9, "Vitamins", "Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are stored in the body; water-soluble vitamins (B vitamins and C) aren't stored well. Deficiencies include scurvy (vitamin C), rickets (vitamin D), night blindness (vitamin A), and neural tube defects (folate). Vitamin K helps clotting.", ["Fat-soluble: A, D, E, K", "Water-soluble: B, C", "Vitamin C → scurvy", "Folate prevents neural tube defects"]),
    ch(10, "Minerals", "Major minerals include calcium (bones, teeth), phosphorus, magnesium, sodium, potassium, and chloride. Trace minerals include iron (hemoglobin; deficiency causes anemia), zinc, iodine (thyroid; goiter), and fluoride (teeth). Too much sodium raises blood pressure.", ["Calcium for bones", "Iron deficiency → anemia", "Iodine → goiter", "Limit sodium"]),
    ch(11, "Water", "Water is the most essential nutrient: it regulates temperature, transports nutrients, and removes waste. Needs increase with heat and activity. Signs of dehydration include thirst, dark urine, fatigue, and dizziness.", ["Most essential nutrient", "Regulates temperature", "Dark urine = dehydration", "Needs rise with activity"]),
    ch(12, "Energy Balance", "Energy balance compares calories eaten to calories used. Energy use includes basal metabolism (largest part), physical activity, and the thermic effect of food. One pound of body fat is about 3,500 calories.", ["Calories in vs. out", "Basal metabolism largest", "Thermic effect of food", "1 lb ≈ 3,500 calories"]),
    ch(13, "Healthy Weight Management", "BMI (weight relative to height) screens weight status. Healthy weight management combines balanced eating, portion control, and physical activity. Fad diets are unsafe or unsustainable. Obesity raises risk of chronic diseases.", ["BMI screening", "Portion control", "Avoid fad diets", "Activity + diet"]),
    ch(14, "Nutrition Across the Life Span", "Needs change with age: pregnancy (folate, iron, extra calories), infancy (breast milk or formula; solids around 6 months), childhood, adolescence (calcium, iron for growth), adulthood, and older adulthood (vitamin D, B12, fewer calories).", ["Pregnancy: folate and iron", "Solids ~6 months", "Teens: calcium and iron", "Older adults: B12, vitamin D"]),
    ch(15, "Eating for Sports Performance", "Athletes need extra calories and carbohydrates (stored as glycogen), adequate protein, and fluids before, during, and after activity. Pre-game meals are eaten 2–4 hours before. Supplements and steroids can be dangerous.", ["Carbs fuel exercise", "Hydrate before, during, after", "Pre-game meal 2–4 hours before", "Avoid risky supplements"]),
    ch(16, "Eating Disorders", "Eating disorders include anorexia nervosa (self-starvation), bulimia nervosa (bingeing and purging), and binge eating disorder. They have serious health effects and need professional treatment. The female athlete triad links low energy, menstrual problems, and bone loss.", ["Anorexia: self-starvation", "Bulimia: binge and purge", "Binge eating disorder", "Seek professional help"]),
    ch(17, "Planning Healthy Meals", "Meal planning considers nutrition, budget, time, and preferences, using MyPlate as a guide. Variety, balance, and moderation are key. Healthy snacks and breakfast support energy and focus.", ["Use MyPlate", "Variety, balance, moderation", "Budget planning", "Healthy snacks"]),
    ch(18, "Shopping for Food", "Smart shopping includes making a list, comparing unit prices, reading labels, choosing store brands, and understanding dates ('sell by,' 'use by'). Organic and other label claims have specific meanings.", ["Shopping lists", "Unit pricing", "Read labels", "Date labels"]),
    ch(19, "Preparing Food", "Healthy cooking methods include baking, broiling, grilling, steaming, and microwaving, which keep nutrients and limit fat. Kitchen safety prevents burns, cuts, and fires. Recipes can be modified to lower fat, sugar, and sodium.", ["Steam to keep nutrients", "Limit frying", "Kitchen safety", "Modify recipes"]),
    ch(20, "Staying Physically Active", "Regular activity improves heart and lung health, strength, flexibility, and mood. Teens need 60 minutes of activity daily; adults 150 minutes of moderate activity weekly. The FITT principle (frequency, intensity, time, type) guides exercise.", ["Teens: 60 min/day", "Adults: 150 min/week", "FITT principle", "Warm up and cool down"]),
    ch(21, "Mental and Social Wellness", "Mental wellness includes self-esteem, coping, and managing emotions. Social wellness involves healthy relationships and communication. Depression and anxiety are treatable; seek help from trusted adults or professionals.", ["Self-esteem", "Healthy relationships", "Seek help", "Coping skills"]),
    ch(22, "Stress and Wellness", "Stress triggers the fight-or-flight response. Chronic stress harms health. Management strategies include exercise, sleep, time management, relaxation, and talking to others.", ["Fight-or-flight", "Chronic stress harms health", "Exercise and sleep", "Time management"]),
    ch(23, "Drug and Supplement Use and Your Health", "Misuse of drugs, alcohol, tobacco, and vaping harms health and can lead to addiction. Dietary supplements aren't regulated like drugs and may be unnecessary or unsafe. Caffeine and energy drinks have risks.", ["Avoid tobacco and vaping", "Alcohol harms", "Supplements loosely regulated", "Energy drink risks"]),
]

